# Defect 2271, 2026-10-29. The `f32` ladder read its round trip through a
# cast, `(float)strtod(...)`, which rounds the decimal twice: `strtod` can land
# a decimal just off a binary32 midpoint exactly on it, a midpoint having 25
# significant bits and so being a double, and the cast's tie-to-even then takes
# the neighbour the decimal is farther from. `7.038531e-26` is 2.2e-42 below the
# midpoint of the binary32s 0x15ae43fd and 0x15ae43fe, so it names 0x15ae43fd;
# the cast read it as 0x15ae43fe, so 0x15ae43fe printed it, a text naming its
# neighbour, and 0x15ae43fd, refused it, printed `7.0385307e-26`. Read back by
# `strtof`, each prints a spelling of its own: `7.0385313e-26` and
# `7.038531e-26`.
#
# Each value is written as its exact decimal, which `atof` reads exactly: an
# `f32` literal is read through a double too, and the short
# `0.00000000000000000000000007038531` would be read as 0x15ae43fe.

function main()
    # 0x15ae43fe: its seven digits name the neighbour below, so it takes eight.
    above: f32 = 0.0000000000000000000000000703853130814879132477466099505324860128273323223169199991389177739620208740234375
    print(above)
    print(-above)
    print(to_str(above))
    print(f"{above}")

    # 0x15ae43fd: the seven digits are its own, which the cast refused it.
    below: f32 = 0.00000000000000000000000007038530691851209120859188017140306974105991300039164570989669300615787506103515625
    print(below)
    print(above == below)
